  "id": "Compound::DB00178 - Disease::DOID:3393",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Ramipril may be associated with Coronary Artery Disease through multiple indirect pathways. Ramipril is linked to increased serum potassium levels, which is mediated by Valsartan or Eplerenone, both of which are antihypertensive agents. These agents, in turn, are associated with Coronary Artery Disease, indicating a potential role in the pathophysiology of the condition. Additionally, Ramipril is associated with an increase in antinuclear antibodies, which is influenced by Gemfibrozil, another antihypertensive agent that is linked to Coronary Artery Disease. The shared antihypertensive properties and the involvement of compounds with organic structures in these pathways suggest a possible mechanism by which Ramipril may contribute to the development of Coronary Artery Disease. The evidence is supported by multiple pathways, but the strength of the association varies, with some pathways showing stronger correlations. The integration of these findings highlights the complex interplay between drug effects and disease mechanisms, emphasizing the need for further research to clarify the precise role of Ramipril in this context.",
